+An assertion inside a test can read the data file as text only where nothing in it is compressed: the run has to
+write `TestRunReport.json`, with payload compression off. `CompressTestRunReportPayloads`, on by default since 4.0.0,
+stores every body and diagram source of 8,192 characters or more gzipped, where a text search cannot see it. It is
+obsolete from 5.0.0 (warning CS0618) until v6 removes it, so the line that turns it off needs the warning suppressed:
 
 ```csharp
+options.CompressTestRunReport = false;         // TestRunReport.json, not the compressed TestRunReport.krkl
+#pragma warning disable CS0618                 // obsolete from 5.0.0
 options.CompressTestRunReportPayloads = false; // every body and diagram stays plain text in the data file
+#pragma warning restore CS0618
 // ... run the tests and write the report ...
 var json = File.ReadAllText(Path.Combine(ReportGenerator.ResolveReportsDirectory(options), "TestRunReport.json"));
 Assert.DoesNotContain("my-secret-token", json);
 ```
 
-The same goes for `CompressTestRunReport` (4.32.0+), which writes the whole file compressed as `TestRunReport.krkl`:
-its bytes hold no text to search, so a text check of them passes whatever the file holds. Check it through
-`kronikol query grep` as above, or write its JSON out with `kronikol decode` and check that.
