 | `GenerateSpecificationsMarkdown` | `bool` | `false` | When `true`, writes `<YamlSpecificationsFileName>.md` — the specification as prose, with no run data at all. Unlike its two siblings it is **not** blanked on a failed run. Suppressed by `ExpectedTestCount` like every other specification output. See [Generated Reports](Generated-Reports#specificationsmd). Since 3.1.0. |
 | `GenerateCtrfReport` | `bool` | `false` | When `true`, writes `ctrf-report.json` — the run in [Common Test Report Format](https://ctrf.io), for CI tooling that already reads it. Carries failure messages and traces verbatim, and is swept up by `PublishCiArtifacts` like every other `.json` in the folder. See [Generated Reports](Generated-Reports#ctrf-reportjson-optional). Since 3.1.0. |
 | `GenerateFailuresDigest` | `bool` | `true` | When `true`, writes `Failures.md` and `Failures.jsonl` beside the report: every failure in context — error, parsed expected/actual, the failing step and its `file:line`, the calls made inside it, attachments, and the query address of each — clustered so that twenty scenarios broken by one cause read as one cause. Never a payload, a header or a diagram. **Written on a green run too**, as `# No failures` (nothing failed **in the newest run**: since 3.27.0, when the run it replaced had failures, the line under the heading says so and where that run is kept, see `KeepRuns`), so that the file's absence always means the run did not finish. See [Generated Reports](Generated-Reports#failuresmd-and-failuresjsonl). Since 3.1.0. |
